WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.870
Very little is known about the gulper eel.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.040
This camera-shy deep sea fish is sometimes

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.460
called the pelican eel because of the strange

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:11.620
things it can do with its mouth.

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.420
Researchers were based out of the Azores Islands

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:16.850
where they have been studying underwater species

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.080
for almost 30 years.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:22.200
They boarded a submarine and plunged 1000

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.060
meters below the surface of the water.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:27.320
Then they waited.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:35.720
This is the first time the pelican eel has

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.960
been seen directly by humans hunting for prey.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.920
An early video from an unmanned submarine

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:49.300
showed the crazy mouth move, but this video

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:51.640
is the first evidence that pelican eels actually

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:54.280
pursue their prey, and don’t just hang out in

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.370
the water waiting for it to fall into their mouths.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:59.720
The scientists hope their footage will help

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:01.870
us to better understand the behavior of the

00:01:01.870 --> 00:01:03.140
pelican eel.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:05.560
And shine some light on how and why it evolved

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:07.420
its balloon-like head.

